n- Files: `createfile`/`readfile`/`writefile`/`deletefile`/`listfiles` plus `touch`/`cat`/`write`/`rm`/`ls` aliases (no-login behavior, basic flow, duplicates, permission-denied, admin override at perm ≥ 5/9, usage, exceeding `MAX_FILES`)\n- System: `help` / `?`, `status`, `debug` / `verbose` toggles\n- Did-you-mean fallbacks for `add*`, `log*`, `list*`, `create*`, `read*`, `write*`, `delete*`, plus the unknown-command path\n- Truncation behavior (token > `MAX_COMMAND`, line > `MAX_INPUT`, more args than `MAX_ARGS`)\n- Mixed whitespace/tab tokenization\n- A composite end-to-end workflow\n\n**Result.** `cargo test`: **42 passed, 0 failed**. The Rust translation produces byte-identical stdout to the C implementation across every tested command and edge case.\n\n**One C UB note (not fixed, by design).** The C `process_command` calls `strlen(command)` on a stack-local `command[MAX_COMMAND]` that is uninitialized when `parse_command` produces no token (empty / whitespace-only input). This is undefined and produces non-deterministic output in the C binary. I avoid feeding empty/whitespace-only lines so deterministic byte-equality holds; this matches the spirit of the rule \"the C code is correct — replicate, do not fix\" because there is no observable, reproducible C behavior to match in that branch."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":737,"cache_read_input_tokens":53326,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":737,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"873f62a5-0fa9-4495-8fbb-a0d5032a7501","uuid":"8cf388a9-38fe-4d20-ac2a-ed5675777136"}
